p.src:
The lecture begins with bots as a familiar annoyance, then quickly reframes them as an accounting problem. If bots can like, retweet, comment, and post, then they do not merely clutter the feed. They enter the scoreboard. A compact reconstruction is $\begin{aligned}
E_{\mathrm{rep}} &= E_{\mathrm{auth}} + E_{\mathrm{bot}},\\
M_{\mathrm{rep}} &= M_{\mathrm{auth}} + M_{\mathrm{bot}},\end{aligned}$ where $E$ denotes engagement and $M$ monthly active users. These are editorial formulas, not quoted board equations, but they preserve the lecture’s logic closely: reported activity can rise even when authentic activity does not.

